nal nesting or grouping (e.g., households) is present.\n\n- How to use in the simulation\n  - Agent initialization: Create one Agent object per row with attributes: demographics (age, age_group, occupation), psychological (risk_perception), and behavioral state (mask_wearing initialized from initial_mask_wearing).\n  - Network construction: For each layer, generate edges to match the degree sequence (e.g., configuration model) and optionally weight layers differently (family > work/school > community) for influence/contact frequency. total_connections can validate sums.\n  - Behavior dynamics: At each step, update mask_wearing based on:\n    - Social influence: proportion of masked neighbors per layer (with layer-specific weights).\n    - Individual trait: risk_perception as a baseline propensity or as a coefficient in an adoption function (e.g., logistic/threshold).\n    - Demographic/occupation modifiers: occupation and age_group can modulate exposure/influence strength in work/school vs. other layers.\n  - Seeding and calibration: initial_mask_wearing defines initial adopters; risk_perception distribution and degree counts shape early adoption and diffusion speed.\n\nSemantic metadata summary for social_network.json\n\n- Data structure and type:\n  - JSON object representing a multilayer social network as adjacency lists.\n  - Top-level: mapping from agent IDs (stringified integers, range appears to be 0–999) to an object of contact lists.\n\n- Meaning of keys:\n  - Each top-level key: a unique agent/person ID.\n  - Nested keys within each agent:\n    - family: list of agent IDs representing familial contacts.\n    - work_school: list of agent IDs representing workplace or school contacts.\n    - community: list of agent IDs representing broader community/social contacts.\n    - all: union of the above lists (appears deduplicated), i.e., all known contacts for that agent.\n\n- Relationships and nested elements:\n  - Each nested list is an adjacency list for that layer; entries are integer IDs referencing other agents.\n  - Edges are given from the perspective of the focal agent; reciprocity is not guaranteed by the data (treat as directed unless symmetrized).\n  - No weights, timestamps, or frequencies are included; no self-loops observed in the sample.\n  - The “all” list is a derived convenience field; it should equal the deduplicated union of family ∪ work_school ∪ community.\n\n- How this informs simulation entities and interactions:\n  - Agents: instantiate one agent per top-level key; store mask-wearing state and any behavioral parameters externally (not in this file).\n  - Network layers: construct three layers (family, work_school, community) to enable layer-specific influence/interaction rules; optionally also an aggregate network from “all.”\n  - Interaction modeling:\n    - Use layer membership to set contact probabilities, frequencies, or influence strengths (e.g., family > work/school > community).\n    - Decide on edge directionality: either use as directed (as-is) or symmetrize for undirected interactions.\n    - Sample daily interactions per agent from the relevant layer lists according to your schedule/policy.\n  - Initialization/validation:\n    - Convert string keys to integers for consistency.\n    - Verify that “all” equals the union of the three layers; recompute if needed.\n    - Check and deduplicate neighbor lists if required by your engine.\n  - Scale: IDs suggest a population of approximately 1,000 agents, suitable for agent-based simulation over multilayer contacts.\n\nSemantic metadata summary for train_data.csv\n\n- Overall structure and type:\n  - CSV file in long-format panel (time-series per agent).\n  - Each row is an agent-day observation; multiple rows per agent across days.\n  - Composite primary key: (agent_id, day).\n\n- Columns and meanings:\n  - day (int): Discrete simulation time index (t = 0,1,2,...).\n  - agent_id (int): Unique identifier for an agent.\n  - wearing_mask (bool): Agent’s behavioral state on that day (true = wearing a mask).\n  - received_info (bool): Whether the agent received pro-mask information on that day (source unspecified).\n\n- Relationships or nested elements:\n  - No nested structures; flat table.\n  - Temporal dependency is implicit: state at day t can depend on states/events at t-1.\n  - No social network topology or neighbor relationships included; info source and contagion pathways are not encoded.\n\n- How this data should inform simulation entities or interactions:\n  - Agent state variables:\n    - wearing_mask_t: behavioral state to initialize or validate against.\n    - info_flag_t: exogenous/endogenous information event indicator; can seed “information memory.”\n  - Transition modeling:\n    - Calibrate per-day transition probabilities P(mask_t | mask_{t-1}, info_t) from the panel:\n      - Adoption: P(mask_t = 1 | mask_{t-1} = 0, info_t).\n      - Persistence: P(mask_t = 1 | mask_{t-1} = 1, info_t).\n      - Reversion: P(mask_t = 0 | mask_{t-1} = 1, info_t = 0).\n      - Spontaneous adoption: P(mask_t = 1 | mask_{t-1} = 0, info_t = 0).\n    - Optionally estimate lags: P(mask_t | info_{t-k}) to capture delayed effects.\n    - Allow heterogeneity by agent_id if data permit (random effects or per-agent propensities).\n  - Simulation initialization:\n    - Use day 0 rows to set initial mask-wearing states; optionally bootstrap info flags if present.\n  - Network dynamics integration:\n    - Since network edges are absent, introduce or import a social graph; model received_info_t as:\n      - Exogenous broadcast process calibrated from observed info rates, and/or\n      - Endogenous diffusion from neighbors (e.g., threshold or weighted influence); fit parameters so that simulated P(mask_t) trajectories match the panel.\n  - Validation targets:\n    - Match aggregate prevalence over time, switching rates, and persistence measured from this file.\n  - Data handling notes:\n    - Booleans stored as true/false; ensure correct parsing.\n    - Check for missing days per agent; if gaps exist, treat as skipped time steps or impute.\n\nCALIBRATION CONTEXT:\n{\n  "task_description": "Develop a multi-agent simulation system that models the spread of mask-wearing behavior through social networks.",\n  "metrics": [],\n  "file_purposes": {}\n}\n\nBased on the provided data summaries and task description, please analyze the data and provide insights for simulation model calibration.\nYour analysis should cover:\n\n1. What key patterns, distributions, and relationships exist in the data?\n2. How should this data be used to calibrate the simulation model?\n3. What parameters can be extracted from the data for configuring the simulation?\n4. What simulation design recommendations would you make based on this data?\n\nProvide your response in the following JSON format:\n```json\n{\n  "data_summary": {\n    "key_patterns": [\n      {"name": "Pattern Name", "description": "Description of the pattern", "relevance": "Why this matters for the simulation"}\n    ],\n    "key_distributions": [\n      {"name": "Distribution Name", "description": "Description of the distribution", "parameters": "Parameters that define this distribution"}\n    ],\n    "key_relationships": [\n      {"variables": ["var1", "var2"], "relationship": "Description of the relationship", "strength": "Description of strength"}\n    ]\n  },\n  "simulation_parameters": {\n    "parameter_category_1": {\n      "parameter_name_1": {\n        "value": "Extracted or recommended value",\n        "source": "Which data file and features this comes from",\n        "confidence": "High/Medium/Low",\n        "notes": "Any additional notes about this parameter"\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  "calibration_strategy": {\n    "preprocessing_steps": [\n      {"step": "Step description", "purpose": "Why this step is necessary"}\n    ],\n    "calibration_approach": "Description of overall approach to calibration",\n    "validation_strategy": "How to validate the calibrated model",\n    "key_variables_to_calibrate": ["var1", "var2", "var3"]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nProvide only valid JSON that can be parsed. Don\'t include any other explanation or text outside the JSON.\n'}]}], 'max_output_tokens': 10000, 'model': 'gpt-5-2025-08-07'}}
